dditional half dose of its vaccine saw  a 37-fold increase in antibody levels a  full dose led to an 83-fold increase  both produced side effects comparable to  those seen after the first two doses now  these lab results are preliminary and  will now undergo a full scientific  review  the omicron variant and the prospect of  another covid christmas are prompting  plenty of questions from viewers to  answer some of those we are joined now  by dr anna banerjee who's an infectious  disease specialist with the university  of toronto dr banerjee thanks so much  for joining us you know we've heard that  news about moderna and the effectiveness  of its booster shot so the first  question for you comes from erna jean  who says that my first two vaccinations  were astrazeneca then she had the pfizer  shot six months later does it matter  what booster shots she receives  so basically i consider  pfizer and modern-like crest and colgate  they're basically the same the the  buffers are slightly different but they  work in the same way so right now  either of the messenger rna vaccines are  fine  okay next question comes from dale who  says that the vaccines that we're  currently using are for the earlier  covid variants of course that's what  they were designed for initially but now  we have a new variant do we need to  start trying to make a new vaccine  altogether  i think that there's some controversy i  mean if you listen to dr fauci in the  states he'll say you know it seems to  work and recently the booster as you  said for moderna seems to cause a good  immune response but some of us are  wondering you know if the vaccine was  designed for the original alpha variant  maybe it's time to uh  to to look at updating the vaccine  especially if it looks like you know  with two shots the the effectiveness is  going down until you get boosted so i i  think that's a question that we'll have  in the future  especially as as mutations occur like  the influenza vaccine maybe it needs to  be updated  yeah and we have heard of course from  moderna and pfizer both with plans to  develop new vaccines to better match the  omicron variant if needed next question  for you comes from karen who asks what  are the symptoms you feel if you test  positive for the omicron variant  well it depends it depends on many  different things uh it depends on how  old you are what your immune status is  like if you've been unvaccinated or not  from what i understand omicron is  similar to delta  and so you can have symptoms for all the  way from no symptoms just a mild  mild runny nose cold like symptoms all  the way to more serious symptoms the  more serious symptoms are  you know if someone's not vaccinated or  someone's compromised and so they  couldn't uh you know take the vaccine as  well so it really depends on on on many  circumstances  okay next question comes from pat asking  about transmissibility so wondering if  the omicron variant is more  transmissible when we touch surfaces for  example or is it mostly airborne spread  what do you think  so there's been some controversy over  this over the years and i've thought  almost from the very beginning that  co-fed was airborne and now more and  more people are saying that it's  airborne so it's really not uh you know  touching surfaces even though you can  get  viruses that way  through droplet spread if someone's  coughing or sneezing  but a lot of people are saying that's  really through these small particles  that linger in the air for a while and  so  and that might impact  you know ventilation systems the kind of  mask we wear but i think that we're  thinking that it's it's uh  more airborne than from touch last year  people worried about grocery bags and  shopping carts and i really think it's  it's more the the air that we're  concerned about dr anna banerjee an  infectious disease specialist from the  university of toronto thanks so much for  tackling some of those questions for us  really appreciate 